1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,442
Pick one AI feature idea, real if you have one at work, realistic if you don't, and

2
00:00:05,442 --> 00:00:10,289
take it all the way from a written spec to a risk-assessed, metric-instrumented

3
00:00:10,289 --> 00:00:14,583
plan, using six artifacts this course has already taught you to build.

4
00:00:14,583 --> 00:00:17,283
Nothing about this capstone is new material.

5
00:00:17,283 --> 00:00:22,313
It's the same assembly-under-a-deadline exercise 10.1 asked of a discovery sprint,

6
00:00:22,313 --> 00:00:27,467
except the deadline is now yours to set and the scope is the whole course instead of

7
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:28,510
one week's tools.

8
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:32,129
Be honest with yourself about the time this actually takes.

9
00:00:32,129 --> 00:00:36,669
This isn't a twenty-minute in-lecture exercise like the section workshops.

10
00:00:36,669 --> 00:00:41,761
Block real time, a weekend is realistic, because six genuine artifacts for one real

11
00:00:41,761 --> 00:00:46,915
feature is genuinely a few hours of work, not something to rush between other tasks.

12
00:00:47,465 --> 00:00:52,369
The next two lectures walk through one worked solution to this exact brief, and the

13
00:00:52,369 --> 00:00:57,155
value is entirely in doing the work yourself before you see anyone else's answer.

14
00:00:57,155 --> 00:00:59,459
Why does the order matter so much here?

15
00:00:59,459 --> 00:01:04,186
Because once you've seen someone else's numbers, you can't honestly tell whether

16
00:01:04,186 --> 00:01:07,022
yours were real judgement or a memory of theirs.

17
00:01:07,022 --> 00:01:11,690
This is also the last point in the course where anyone hands you the structure.

18
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:15,294
Every artifact until now came with a lecture built around it.

19
00:01:15,294 --> 00:01:19,903
From here, the only brief is this one, which is deliberately closer to how the

20
00:01:19,903 --> 00:01:23,448
actual job works than anything else in this course has been.

21
00:01:23,998 --> 00:01:29,363
These six are required regardless of what feature you pick, because every AI feature

22
00:01:29,363 --> 00:01:34,728
needs the same six answers: what it is, whether it works, what it costs, which model

23
00:01:34,728 --> 00:01:40,092
earns its place, what could go wrong, and how you'll know it's working after launch.

24
00:01:40,092 --> 00:01:44,180
That's the whole course, compressed to one page of deliverables.

25
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:48,203
Two more artifacts join the list conditionally, not by default.

26
00:01:48,203 --> 00:01:53,568
Add 5.7's RAG design doc and 5.4's retrieval scorecard only if your feature actually

27
00:01:53,568 --> 00:01:55,612
retrieves from a knowledge base.

28
00:01:55,612 --> 00:01:59,508
Add 6.8's agent worksheet only if it takes autonomous action.

29
00:01:59,508 --> 00:02:04,745
Forcing either one into a feature that doesn't need it is exactly the mistake 10.5

30
00:02:04,745 --> 00:02:09,790
warned against: padding a package with artifacts that don't belong to the story

31
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:11,323
you're actually telling.

32
00:02:11,323 --> 00:02:16,560
Notice, too, this list deliberately doesn't include every artifact this course has

33
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:17,135
produced.

34
00:02:17,135 --> 00:02:22,436
A03 and A04, the two eval harness formats, are tools for building A02, not separate

35
00:02:22,436 --> 00:02:23,266
deliverables.

36
00:02:23,266 --> 00:02:25,310
Building the golden set matters.

37
00:02:25,310 --> 00:02:30,483
Which spreadsheet or script you used to score it doesn't need its own line in the

38
00:02:30,483 --> 00:02:30,994
package.

39
00:02:31,544 --> 00:02:32,575
Build all six now.

40
00:02:32,575 --> 00:02:34,637
Learn which four to lead with later.

41
00:02:35,187 --> 00:02:39,864
This is the actual signal worth optimising for, and it's not whether any single

42
00:02:39,864 --> 00:02:42,351
artifact looks correct sitting on its own.

43
00:02:42,351 --> 00:02:47,265
It's whether all six agree with each other, because that agreement is only possible

44
00:02:47,265 --> 00:02:51,409
if you held the whole feature in your head while building every piece.

45
00:02:51,409 --> 00:02:54,843
Check this yourself before you call the capstone finished.

46
00:02:54,843 --> 00:02:59,816
If your PRD promises ninety-five percent and your golden set's pass rate is measured

47
00:02:59,816 --> 00:03:04,493
against ninety, that's not a rounding difference, it's a package that never got

48
00:03:04,493 --> 00:03:05,558
reread end to end.

49
00:03:05,558 --> 00:03:10,295
The same check applies to volume: if the cost model assumes ten thousand monthly

50
00:03:10,295 --> 00:03:15,090
uses and the PRD never mentions that number anywhere, one of the two documents is

51
00:03:15,090 --> 00:03:19,234
wrong, and finding out which is exactly the work this exercise is for.

52
00:03:19,234 --> 00:03:24,089
This consistency check costs almost nothing compared to building the six artifacts

53
00:03:24,089 --> 00:03:28,766
in the first place, and it's the part most people skip under deadline pressure.

54
00:03:28,766 --> 00:03:31,312
Spend the last twenty minutes on it anyway.

55
00:03:31,312 --> 00:03:35,101
It's the highest-leverage twenty minutes in the entire exercise.

56
00:03:35,651 --> 00:03:40,548
Say the honest caveat before you open the next two lectures, because a capstone with

57
00:03:40,548 --> 00:03:45,445
exactly one right answer would teach the opposite lesson from everything this course

58
00:03:45,445 --> 00:03:46,436
has built toward.

59
00:03:46,436 --> 00:03:51,275
If your cost model assumes a different volume, your risk register names a different

60
00:03:51,275 --> 00:03:56,172
top concern, or your feature idea is nothing like the worked example's, none of that

61
00:03:56,172 --> 00:03:57,688
is a deviation to correct.

62
00:03:57,688 --> 00:04:02,469
It's what real judgement looks like applied to a different feature under different

63
00:04:02,469 --> 00:04:03,168
assumptions.

64
00:04:03,168 --> 00:04:07,075
The test was never whether you match the worked solution's numbers.

65
00:04:07,075 --> 00:04:11,972
It's whether you can defend your own, under a follow-up question, the exact standard

66
00:04:11,972 --> 00:04:13,837
10.6 asked of an interview room.

67
00:04:13,837 --> 00:04:18,152
Use the worked solution the way 4.9 taught you to use a second rater: as a

68
00:04:18,152 --> 00:04:22,990
calibration check on your own reasoning, not a key to mark yourself against line by

69
00:04:22,990 --> 00:04:23,282
line.

70
00:04:23,832 --> 00:04:28,858
Do this before you move on to anything else, because the feature choice is the one

71
00:04:28,858 --> 00:04:33,701
decision every other artifact depends on, and it's the easiest step to postpone

72
00:04:33,701 --> 00:04:35,724
indefinitely if you let yourself.

73
00:04:35,724 --> 00:04:39,831
Pick something substantial enough to need all six answers honestly.

74
00:04:39,831 --> 00:04:44,735
A feature so simple that the risk register has nothing to say and the cost model

75
00:04:44,735 --> 00:04:49,638
rounds to zero won't give you a real capstone, it'll give you six thin documents

76
00:04:49,638 --> 00:04:53,990
about a feature that never needed this much process in the first place.

77
00:04:53,990 --> 00:04:58,894
If nothing from your own work fits, borrow one of this course's running examples

78
00:04:58,894 --> 00:05:03,737
instead of inventing something from nothing: a support ticket triage feature, a

79
00:05:03,737 --> 00:05:06,863
contract review assistant, a refund approval agent.

80
00:05:06,863 --> 00:05:12,012
Any of them carries enough real stakes to need every artifact on the list, honestly.

81
00:05:12,562 --> 00:05:14,267
One thing before you move on.

82
00:05:14,267 --> 00:05:19,148
Name the feature in writing, today, even if the rest of the package takes until the

83
00:05:19,148 --> 00:05:19,618
weekend.

84
00:05:19,618 --> 00:05:22,323
Then block the time honestly on your calendar.

85
00:05:22,323 --> 00:05:27,204
Six real artifacts don't happen in the gaps between other work, and pretending they

86
00:05:27,204 --> 00:05:30,144
will is how a capstone quietly never gets started.

87
00:05:30,144 --> 00:05:34,965
Next lecture is the worked solution, part one: spec and evals, walking through one

88
00:05:34,965 --> 00:05:38,141
way to answer this exact brief, starting with the PRD.
